"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[1533],{11533:(e,t,a)=>{a.d(t,{L:()=>n,V:()=>o});let n=[{version:"1.0.0",date:"2026-08-05",summary:"First release of the packages as publishable artefacts, MIT licensed: WCAG 2.2 AA enforced across every route with four Level AAA criteria gated in CI, a database-backed permissions demo on `/live`, and a toolchain on TypeScript 7.",breaking:["Icons moved from lucide-react to Phosphor, re-exported through a single `@inclusive/react/icons` vocabulary. Import names are unchanged where an equivalent existed; `lucide-react` is no longer a dependency.","`@inclusive/core/tailwind` now resolves to built JavaScript instead of raw TypeScript source. Consumers no longer need to transpile it — and previously could not import it at all outside this monorepo.","Removed a duplicate `ErrorState` from `atoms` that no barrel exported. The `molecules` one (`title`/`description`) is and always was the public component.","`Sheet` now names its dialog from `SheetTitle`. A sheet with no `SheetTitle` should pass `aria-label`.","Packages move from the placeholder `0.0.1` to `1.0.0`. Earlier entries below record documentation-site milestones from before the packages were versioned."],features:["**The packages are MIT licensed and publish publicly.** `@inclusive/tokens`, `@inclusive/core` and `@inclusive/react` each ship their own `LICENSE.md`, verified present in all three tarballs. The repository had been `UNLICENSED` with a proprietary licence file forbidding distribution to third parties, while the same packages were configured to publish — the two could not both hold.",'A real back end on **one** ATS route — `/live`: Neon Postgres, server-enforced permissions, an append-only audit trail and live multi-user updates over Server-Sent Events. The other fifteen ATS routes remain a client-side demo on `localStorage`, which is a scope decision recorded in `Documentation/BACKEND.md`. This entry previously read "a real back end for the ATS demo", which claimed more than one route\'s worth.',"Field-level authorisation on `/live` — a hiring manager's job JSON contains no salary keys at all, rather than hiding them in the UI. Asserted in `e2e/api-backend.spec.ts`.","Persona switching across three roles (admin, recruiter, hiring manager) so the permission model can be demonstrated rather than described. The switcher mounts only on `/live`, so no route can show a persona's role while serving it unfiltered `localStorage` data.","`npm run pkg:check` gates the published packages with publint and @arethetypeswrong/cli, so a package that builds but does not resolve after install fails the build.","Chromatic wired into CI for component-level visual review; skips silently until `CHROMATIC_PROJECT_TOKEN` is set.","Every one of the 117 catalogued components now has a Storybook story and a unit test — previously 93 and 99 respectively.","A changelog that is generated and gated, replacing one hardcoded in a page component."],fixes:["`Sheet` rendered a modal dialog with no accessible name, affecting every sheet in the system including the mobile navigation (WCAG 4.1.2).","`MobileMenu` signalled the current destination by colour alone when items were driven by `onClick` rather than `href` (WCAG 1.4.1).","`AlertDialog` nested two dialog roles; the outer one — the one carrying `aria-modal` — had no accessible name.","`RichTextEditor`'s toolbar active states never updated: it read the DOM during render with no state and no effects, so Bold and Italic only ever lit up by coincidence.","`Lightbox` threw for any consumer rendering it outside a React Aria `OverlayProvider`, so it could not be used standalone.","`DatePicker` never called `preventDefault` for Enter, Space or Escape — Space scrolled the page and Enter could submit an enclosing form.",'`DatePicker` rendered `role="grid"` with day buttons as direct children, an invalid ARIA structure.',"The collapsed navigation rail's logo was an unlabelled icon-only link.","A 1.16:1 contrast failure on the settings avatar, exposed when tailwind-merge 3 correctly resolved a background override that version 2 had masked.","`useServiceWorker` returned its registration from a ref, so nothing re-rendered when it arrived.","`CommandMenu` rendered no commands at all: its list showed children only once items existed, but items register from an effect inside those children. Filtering also hid nothing, and clicking a command left the palette open while Enter closed it.","`Button` silently erased `aria-pressed`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-haspopup`, `aria-controls` and `aria-current`. `aria-label` survived, which is why it went unnoticed — every toggle, disclosure and menu trigger built on it lost its state.","`Checkbox` ignored a caller's `aria-label`, leaving every checkbox named only that way unnamed; and accepted `checked` in its type while reading nothing, so the box rendered ticked and clicking it fired nothing.","Table sorting in `DataTable` and `CandidateList` was a click handler on the `<th>` with no tabindex or key handling — unreachable without a mouse (WCAG 2.1.1, Level A). Both also now announce their sort direction.","`EvaluationForm` showed nothing at all when a submit failed, and `JobForm` showed nothing when an empty form was rejected. Both looked like the button had been ignored.","`Sidebar` nested the favourite toggle inside the item's own control (axe: nested-interactive), and the toggle was invisible until hovered with no focus state.","`DropdownMenu` replaced each item's own `onSelect` rather than calling it, so the documented way to give a menu item an action did nothing.","`SheetDescription` reached assistive tech through nothing — the dialog carried no `aria-describedby`.","Toggle groups, timeline filters, the interview scheduler's four selection controls and the team calendar's view switcher all signalled their state by background colour alone (WCAG 1.4.1, 4.1.2).",'Four roles corrected that promised behaviour their component did not implement: a table claiming `role="grid"`, radios with no `radiogroup`, carousel dots claiming to be tabs with no tabpanels, and a decorative tick left in the accessibility tree.',"Six props were accepted and silently ignored: `DataTable`'s `selectionMode=\"single\"`, `ResumeViewer`'s `onClose`, `ApplicationTimeline`'s `onEventClick`, `PipelineStage`'s `assignee`, `CandidateComparison`'s `showExperience` (whose data was a *required* field), and `TimePicker`'s focus contract behind `aria-modal`.","Ten separate `Date.now()` id generators could collide within a millisecond and produce duplicate React keys.",'Documentation across the repository described HeroUI as the component library it is built on — in the `@inclusive/react` barrel, the atoms and molecules barrels, the shipped `globals.css`, the `Dialog` and `AlertDialog` headers, the site\'s search keywords and the public roadmap, which listed "HeroUI integration" as complete. HeroUI was evaluated in January 2026 and never adopted; it is not a dependency of any package.',"`@inclusive/core` carried a second colour pipeline for HeroUI — an `oklch()` to hex converter, a recursive `hexifyColors` walk and a `herouiThemes` export. Nothing imported any of it, and it was the one place that deliberately converted tokens to hex, against the rule that `@inclusive/tokens` is the single source of colour. None of it was in the public surface, so nothing consuming the package changes.","Five one-shot refactor scripts in `packages/react/scripts` still emitted `lucide-react` imports and called `unlinkSync` on their input. The refactor they performed finished months ago, they now sliced line ranges that no longer exist, and running one would have corrupted the source tree.",'A `Modal` test asserted "Escape fired onClose, **or** the close button did" behind an `if`, so it could not fail on a broken Escape key. Escape does work; the test now proves it.',"A 520 KB ESLint JSON dump was committed by accident in a checkpoint commit, carrying absolute filesystem paths from one machine.","The CI job ran `syncpack list-mismatches`, a command removed in syncpack 14; the installed version is 15.3.2. The step carried no `continue-on-error`, so it hard-failed every run. Migrated to `syncpack lint`, which reports no issues.",'All three packages carried `publishConfig.access: "restricted"`. Scoped packages default to that, and it would have required a paid npm plan while contradicting the MIT licence they now ship under. Now `public`.',"The Figma plugin's manifest had no `license` and no `private` field, so it was publishable to npm by default — where it would be meaningless, since Figma plugins are distributed through Figma. Now `private: true`.","`project/README.md` was a scaffold stub: empty Description and Example sections, and an install command (`npm install inclusive`) for a package that does not exist.","**Every stated requirement for installing the library was wrong.** The installation page asked for React 18, Node 18 and Tailwind 3.4; the developers page listed Tailwind as an optional 3.4+ extra. The real peers are React 19, Tailwind 4 and Node 20, and Tailwind is required, not optional — so following the published requirements produced a failed install rather than a warning. All now read from the manifests.","The About page claimed React 18 and a Next.js 15 documentation site; both are a major version behind what is installed.","Zero vulnerabilities, dev tree included. A high-severity `tmp` advisory (arbitrary temp file write via symlink, plus path traversal) and a moderate `uuid` one reached the tree through `@lhci/cli`, which is already on its latest release — so npm's only offered remedy was downgrading it three majors to 0.1.0. Pinned through `overrides` instead, and `lhci healthcheck` verified afterwards.",'The ATS settings page said demo data lives in "the shared store" without saying where. It now states plainly that changes stay in your browser, that every person in the dataset is fictional, and that `/live` is the one exception.',"**266 scrollable regions could not be reached by keyboard on a phone-sized viewport — WCAG 2.1.1 Keyboard, Level A.** A container with `overflow: auto` scrolls with a wheel, a trackpad or a finger, but not with a keyboard unless it can take focus. A keyboard-only user could not read anything that had overflowed: the right-hand half of a wide code sample, the columns past the fold of a table, the ATS report tab strip. Measured on a Pixel 5 viewport, **270 of 354 route/theme combinations failed**. The new `ScrollableRegionFocus` measures each region and gives a `tabindex` only to those that have actually overflowed, re-measuring on resize, on DOM change, and once fonts have loaded — a blanket `tabindex` would add dead tab stops on every wide screen. 270 failures down to 8.","**The Cmd/Ctrl+K search dialog could not be closed with Escape once focus left its input.** Escape was handled by the input's own `onKeyDown`, and the input is focused behind a 100 ms timeout — so Escape did nothing in the window before focus landed, and nothing at all after tabbing to a result. A modal you could open with the keyboard and not close with it (WCAG 2.1.2). It now listens on `document` while open, which is the standard modal contract. The dialog had **no e2e coverage of any kind**; it does now.","The search dialog is 1,361 lines and imports a 27.8 KB generated index, was mounted into the design-system layout on every route, and its first statement is `if (!isOpen) return null`. It now loads on first open via `next/dynamic`. The chunk genuinely leaves the document, though first-load JS moved only 1 KB because webpack rebalances a similar volume back in — the honest analysis is in `Documentation/BEST_IN_CLASS_PLAN_PROGRESS.md`. Importing it through the `@/components/navigation` barrel would have split nothing, since the barrel also exports the eagerly-imported `SidebarNav`.",'**The public roadmap was almost entirely untrue.** It advertised the current version as `2.0.0-beta.1` while the packages are `1.0.0`; dated its next two milestones "Q1 2026" and "Q2 2026", both months past; listed seven shipped things as planned, including the CI pipeline, the visual regression baselines and the Storybook coverage; credited the rewrite to DaisyUI, which has never been a dependency — the same fiction that had HeroUI named as the component library in 29 files; and attached vote counts to six "future ideas" nobody has ever voted on, a descending run of 42, 38, 35, 31, 28 and 24 that reads as community demand. The version now derives from the changelog so it cannot drift again, the outstanding work is listed undated, and the vote counts are gone.',"The site carried **three different version histories** — the roadmap, the About page and the changelog — and no two agreed. About claimed a 1.0.0 in 2024 and a 1.5.0 in 2025, neither of which exists anywhere else. Only the changelog is generated from data and gated by CI, so the other two now defer to it.",'The designers\' getting-started page offered to "Browse all 74+ components". There are **117**, counted from the catalogue: 37 atoms, 32 molecules, 22 organisms, 26 ATS. The figure had been left behind by everything added since.','**A hydration failure that only existed near midnight.** `/interviews` failed React #418 in CI at 23:52 UTC. The seeded interviews are built relative to `new Date()`, so the server and the client each computed their own "now", straddled midnight, and disagreed about which day an interview fell on — the date group headers then rendered different text either side of hydration. It cannot reproduce on a machine in BST at a civilised hour, which is why it read as a webkit-only flake and was nothing of the sort. The date-grouped list is now gated on mount, as the upcoming-this-week list on the same page already was.',"**358 contrast violations that were not contrast violations.** axe reported ratios like 1.03:1 on the toast page — near-white text on a near-white background, which the page does not have. Every colour here resolves through `oklch()`, and browsers return the unresolved `oklch(0.98 0.008 85)` string from `getComputedStyle` rather than an sRGB triple, so axe must resolve it itself and under parallel load gets it wrong. Measured: a full webkit run passes 340/340 while tight batches of the same routes fail 2 to 6 of 8. `color-contrast` and `color-contrast-enhanced` are now disabled in the e2e suite — not a lowered bar, the same bar somewhere it can be measured. `npm run contrast:check` computes 360 sanctioned pairings from the token source across four themes with alpha compositing at 7:1, which is stricter than the AA rule dropped and never asks a browser to resolve a colour. The residual gap, recorded on the conformance page: colours outside the token layer are no longer contrast-checked at runtime.","The e2e projects were `chromium`, `firefox`, `webkit` and `mobile-chrome` locally but **only `chromium` and `webkit` in CI**, so nothing narrower than a desktop was ever checked. That is why the defect above survived a full AAA sweep: it cannot fire on a wide viewport, because nothing overflows there. `mobile-chrome` now runs in CI — it emulates a Pixel 5 on the chromium binary, so it costs no extra download.","**73 files under `/design-system` animated regardless of `prefers-reduced-motion`.** Framer Motion defaults to `reducedMotion=\"never\"`, so honouring the media query is opt-in through `MotionConfig` — and `MotionConfig` was mounted only in the ATS layout. The documentation site, including the page that documents reduced-motion support, moved for users who had asked their operating system not to move things. It is now mounted in the design-system layout, the ATS layout and `/why-inclusive` — every place motion actually runs. Not at the app root: `MotionConfig` wraps Framer Motion's own, so it pulls the library in, and at the root that put framer-motion on the critical path of `/`, a page with no motion at all, taking it from 197 KB to 247 KB first-load JS. Measured on `/design-system/typography`: the section's transform previously ran 20px → 15.6 → 12.4 → 8.1 under reduced motion and now drops to `none` immediately. The opacity fade continues by design — Framer Motion suppresses transform and layout while deliberately keeping opacity and colour, since a fade is not a vestibular trigger.",'**`/why-inclusive` carried fabricated content and has been rewritten.** Three testimonials with five-star ratings, attributed to named people at named companies who do not exist. A five-column table giving Greenhouse, Lever and Workable specific unmeasured figures for page load, WCAG conformance and time-to-schedule. Four headline stats — 70% faster hiring, 3x engagement, 50% time saved, "100% Accessible / AAA" — none measured, and the last one false. A closing line about "hundreds of forward-thinking companies that have already made the switch", of which there are none. Every claim on the page is now produced by a gate in this repository, the competitor columns are gone, and the testimonials are replaced by links to the conformance statement, the changelog and the source.','**"WCAG 2.2 AAA compliant" appeared on 29 components** — in JSDoc that ships in the package source and renders into Storybook\'s generated docs, plus two rendered strings. It was not true of any component, and cannot be true of the system while three Level AAA criteria are carried as exceptions. All 29 now read "WCAG 2.2 AA conformant; four Level AAA criteria enforced in CI".','The design principles page claimed "7:1 contrast ratio for normal text (exceeds AAA requirements)". 7:1 **is** the AAA requirement for normal text (1.4.6); it does not exceed it. The same page said "We target WCAG 2.2 AAA, the highest level of accessibility compliance", presented a fabricated quotation to "Amazon Research" that had fused an invented sentence onto a real Greg Linden finding about 100ms of latency, and cited the WHO\'s superseded 15% / 1 billion disability figure rather than the current 16% / 1.3 billion.','Four action buttons lost their accessible name entirely below the `sm` breakpoint (WCAG 4.1.2). Their only text sat in `<span className="hidden sm:inline">`, so on a phone the button was an icon with an `aria-hidden` glyph and nothing else — Filter and Add Candidate on `/candidates`, Schedule Interview on `/interviews`, Refresh on `/reports`. All nine such labels across the ATS now use `sr-only sm:not-sr-only sm:inline`, which looks identical at every width but keeps the name in the accessibility tree. That pattern was already in use in the talent pool; it just had not been applied consistently.','**The conformance page contradicted itself.** It published 2.5.5 Target Size (Enhanced) and 1.3.6 Identify Purpose as known exceptions while its own Level AAA register, on the same page, marked both `met`. For 2.5.5 the register\'s evidence field ended "meeting AA 2.5.8 but not AAA 2.5.5 in the sm size" underneath a `met` label. Both are now `exempted`, so the register and the exception list agree, and `specs/06-quality/01-ACCESSIBILITY.md` — which said 3 exempted all along — is corroborated rather than contradicted. The published figure moves from 30 of 31 AAA criteria met, enforced or not applicable, to **28 of 31 with three exempted**.',"The conformance statement now says what the method does **not** cover: automated tooling finds roughly half of WCAG issues, no screen-reader pass is published, there is no third-party audit or VPAT, and there has been no testing with disabled users. It also states plainly that meeting 28 of 31 AAA criteria is not a Level AAA conformance claim, since conformance at AAA requires every applicable criterion and W3C does not recommend AAA as a site-wide target."],improvements:["TypeScript 7.0 type-checks the repository; `typescript` resolves to 6.0.3 for the tools that still need the JavaScript compiler API.","Packages build with tsdown (rolldown) instead of tsup, which cannot generate declarations on TypeScript 7. Declarations stay bundled — the unbundled alternative produced 257 imports that do not resolve under node16 ESM.","Storybook 10, Vitest 4, ESLint 10, jsdom 29, jest-axe 11, tailwind-merge 3.","Unit tests: 650 → 1,735. Branch coverage 47% → 80%, enforced by a CI threshold. Only ~2% of the branches left uncovered are genuinely unreachable; past roughly 85% the rest are fallback chains where each extra test asserts nothing a user could observe.","Zero production vulnerabilities.",'Visual regression no longer runs in serial mode, where one flaky snapshot marked the following 58 tests as "did not run".',"The bundle budget gate counted Next's `noModule` polyfill bundle, which only browsers without ES modules download. Every route it has ever reported was ~39 KB high; ceilings are re-baselined and headroom narrowed from 8% to 4%.","`npm run budget:check` explains that it needs a running server instead of printing a `fetch failed` stack trace.","New gate: `npm run case:check` resolves every relative import against git's exact-case file list. Windows and macOS resolve `./Button` to `button.tsx` without complaint and Linux does not, so a mis-cased import builds clean on a developer machine and fails the Vercel deploy with `Module not found`. 1,288 source files, currently clean.","A `.env.example` records every environment variable, what it switches on, and how the site behaves without it. There was none, and the answer matters: **nothing is required to build or run the site** — verified from a clean clone with none set. `DATABASE_URL` switches on `/live`; without it that one route says so plainly and the other fifteen are unaffected.","New gate: `npm run links:check` verifies every relative link and heading anchor across all 81 tracked markdown files. Nothing checked the prose documentation at all before — the slug gate covers the site's routes and the e2e sweep covers rendered pages — which is how the specification index spent seven months pointing at a file that has never existed.","The `specs/` directory is now labelled as what it is: design specifications written in January 2026, before the build, never reconciled with it. It specifies a `src/components/` tree, a `providers/` directory, an `apps/storybook/` app and a `tooling/` directory, none of which exist, and an import path (`@inclusive/react/components/atoms`) that does not resolve. The master index lists every known divergence and points at the authoritative source for each. Two sections were brought current instead, because a wrong build config is copy-pasted and then breaks a build."]},{version:"2.1.0",date:"2026-01-12",preRelease:!0,features:["New hierarchical information architecture for documentation","Persistent sidebar navigation with expand/collapse","Global search with Cmd/Ctrl+K keyboard shortcut","Interactive playground powered by Sandpack (CodeSandbox)","Design tokens documentation page with live previews","Persona-based getting started pages (Developer/Designer)","Component documentation template with 8 standardised sections","Marketing landing page redesign with comparison table","Mobile navigation drawer with slide animations"]},{version:"2.0.0",date:"2025-11-20",preRelease:!0,breaking:["Renamed several component props for consistency","Dropped support for the legacy theme API"],features:["Glass Cosmos aesthetic across every surface","Full dark-mode support","ATS-specific component set"]},{version:"2.0.0-rc.3",date:"2025-10-02",preRelease:!0,fixes:["Focus-visible outlines on custom controls","Motion-reduction handling"]},{version:"2.0.0-rc.2",date:"2025-09-08",preRelease:!0,improvements:["Token naming aligned to DTCG","Reduced bundle size"]},{version:"2.0.0-rc.1",date:"2025-08-14",preRelease:!0,features:["First release candidate of the V2 component library"]},{version:"1.0.0",date:"2025-06-15",preRelease:!0,features:["Initial V1 release","Basic component library","Standard light/dark themes"]}],o=n.find(e=>!e.preRelease).version}}]);